:question/math-academy-id "e-9941"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048669}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518544} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566220}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "9826454e-71bd-5e12-b748-06f49a3ff03f" :step/math-academy-id 37429 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130009}}] :activity/title "Graphing General Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129382}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573978} :activity/id #uuid "0e91ff51-fdb7-5a21-aa02-e2fb5d7385c1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050601 :topic/id #uuid "577aebce-a9af-5bcd-867f-fb32f559c852" :topic/math-academy-id 2049} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f042d87-311e-5464-8485-e9af5a7719e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=(x+4)^{2}(x+1)^{5}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573441} {:answer/id #uuid "97d403e6-dd58-5952-92f3-580b8537d460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=(x+4)^{5}(x+1)(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573622} {:answer/id #uuid "a649ff74-4844-5064-99fc-cb9e490009f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=(x+4)^{3}(x+1)^{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573639} {:answer/id #uuid "d75ad061-2732-52c2-b37b-f6ed0b89d899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=(x+4)^{5}(x+1)^{7}(x-1)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573710} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f6a69f-2243-5e6c-bfcd-ac426819c158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=(x+4)(x+1)^{5}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573639} :answer-field/id #uuid "10075deb-baaa-5006-a907-017450fcc1a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f84a89a-22f6-5da0-a06c-36c39480c95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80349" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2ca762ee3bab6480b69edbea41047f6306dfc049d79db9783075a2c5108deb1d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-4$, $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=-4,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-4,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=-1,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=1,$ this root is also a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=(x+4)^{3}(x+1)^{3}(x-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "5535c7dc-0b9b-57be-b146-1efb153a5b00" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033532f8-562f-585e-b821-506ddf21363e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the given graphs are correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573425} {:answer/id #uuid "202ec15a-e02f-52e9-8a2b-9baee9d073d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{I}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573458} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffd1425-4786-5560-b15a-c24f76838f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{III}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573485} {:answer/id #uuid "535cc1cc-8042-5173-82de-c37e04ee3c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{IV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573527} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ae7018-0ed9-5c31-8e4f-ed95fbcd8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{II}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573527} :answer-field/id #uuid "044f5116-22ef-5b62-b853-6b6534655db2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1d7f099c-ef0d-57d9-9c76-6ca878f1638e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80354" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bcc2f587bff8042ea087d8a3ba3ea8be5d7de513cdc3bd37f2a1cafdb4e278a.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-x(x+1)(x+3)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=-1,0$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=-1,0$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-3$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{IV}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{IV}.$"} :step/id #uuid "80f6544c-ec9f-5d9f-ae0c-3175436d094d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433015c8-b02a-5d3b-9bc4-7270edca0b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+1)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345929} {:answer/id #uuid "444cadbf-3703-59f5-bad4-46404cc1a695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(x+1){(x-2)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345940} {:answer/id #uuid "654ada61-08a2-5d34-998b-5c8db9d07e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346234} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbb7820-ea22-5b52-910b-269aa2f0dbbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{5}{(x+1)}^{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346310} {:answer/id #uuid "f282e323-500b-5dcf-a254-2d713e281555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x{(x+1)}^{2}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346234} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5873ca-3084-51bf-9b1d-3de807002492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad55c8cb-7248-5482-9f28-0afc365f8908" :question/math-academy-id "q-80348" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b086759a25af7e464c0c8be78d36cd4d8458c5c25e7c84cb6ecb28b2d59e9df.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-1$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e68a9faa-5c59-5aac-a381-fd3b10fd9ef1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573962}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640fff7e-3c3e-5654-94b0-739b742753ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the given graphs are correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346219} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc5c9a6-da13-59bf-b029-2d82665fe7d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{I}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347007} {:answer/id #uuid "e9163b63-2f51-5de6-b0fe-0d54cfd33629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{IV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347408} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6d6cf5-316e-52ee-9ef0-363ca45cdc83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{III}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347463} {:answer/id #uuid "fa018a1a-74a0-501f-9e53-9af62aa874d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{II}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347463} :answer-field/id #uuid "3392084f-8ea8-519d-871f-fbee9b98158f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "33b39cd4-c03b-51ce-be6d-3b47ccd122f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80361" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bf56fdb429d050465875f1c885aa83a36fb4fd7bfe4709fcda11db1a3574a2d.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-{x}^{4}(x+1)(x-2{)}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0$ is $4,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=-1$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $3,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=2$ is a triple-root, the graph must be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{III}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{III}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{III}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e81252f5-a79f-5a66-9715-128f2f10ec6d"}] :activity/title "Graphing General Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573412}]}
